,ll 5.80,cs nDBACK              SYSTEM MANAGEMENT UTIITY COMMAND                 DBACK,cebackup files to discSYNTAX:  DBACK [-options] <fromdir> [-options] <todir> [-options]Usage: Dback [-options] <fromdir> <todir> [-options]     -b=<no>k= copy buffer      -d=file = backup selected directory      -e      = execute          -f=xxx  = omit files suffixed xxx (default:_BAK)     -k      = omit OS9Boot files      -l      = no recursion     -p      = pause before copying      -s=file = start copying at      -t=file = terminate copying at      -v      = verify        -?      = report help Defaults  source directory (default =  . )  target directory (default = /d0) DEFAULTS: <Fromdir>, the source directory, defaults to the current directory.          <Todir>, the target directory, defaults to /d0. FUNCTION: The program will search the directory <fromdir>, comparing the files and subordinate directories in <fromdir> with those in <todir>.  For all new, or updated files. it will generate copy and makdir commands to copy the files across to <todir>, overwriting any old copies on <todir>.   The program may be used to maintain backup copies of a system, or to assist in rebuilding a system form backups.   As the program will never overwrite a file except by a newer version of itself, it is safer to use than COPY with the -r option.OPTIONS:   The following options are provided:     -b=<no>k  The -b option acts in the same way as it does on the COPY and DSAVE commands.  It specifies the size of the copy buffer.     -d=<file>  Up to 50 -d options may be entered, each one specifies a directory in <fromdir> which is to be backed up (along with its subordinate directories).  If any -d options are specified, then only those directories specified will be copied from <fromdir>.  If none are specified, then the whole of <fromdir> is backed up.     -e  The MAKDIR and COPY commands are normally written to the stdout file.  If the -e option is given then the MAKDIR and COPY commands are implemented immediately.        -f=<suffix>  Up to 20 -f options maty be entered.  Files ending with <suffix> (default is "_BAK") will not be backed up.     -k  If the -k option is given, then any files with the name OS9Boot will be omitted from the backup.     -l  If the -l option is given then no lower level directories are processed, only those specified directly by the <fromdir> and any -d parameters.     -p  This option is designed for use when the backup is being done as a batch job from a shell script.  If the -p option is specified then the program will ask for confirmation before proceeding with the backup.  If a "PORT" environment variable exists, then that will be used to read the confirmation from.  If no "PORT" environment exists, then the pathname "/TERM" is used.      -s=file and -t=file  The -s (start) and -t (termination) parameters allow the user to specify a range of files to be copied.  All files with a pathname (relative to <fromdir>) greater than or equal to the start filename, and less than the termination filename will be copied.  This enables the backing up of dirctories which contain more than a disc full of data.  Only one -s and one -t parameter can be given.  The -s and -t are not allowed with the -d option.      -v  This is the same parameter as used in the COPY command, it verifies the copy command.     -?  This is the normal help utility.EXAMPLE:  The following shows how DBACK may be used to make a script file for a backup job.echo Please load Cmds disc 1dback -b=20k /h0/CMDS /d1/CMDS -p      -t=e  -eecho Please load Cmds disc 2dback -b=20k /h0/CMDS /d1/CMDS -p -s=e -t=q  -eecho Please load Cmds disc 3dback -b=20k /h0/CMDS /d1/CMDS -p -s=q       -eecho Please load System disc 1dback -b=20k /h0 /d1 -p -e -d=PD   -d=PRINTERS    -d=SCULPTORecho Please load System disc 2dback -b=20k /h0 /d1 -p -e -d=DEFS  -d=LIB   -d=MODULES  -d=SYSdback -b=20k /h0 /d1    -e -n -lEcho Please load User disc 1dback -b=20k /h0 /d1 -p -e -d=LETTERS -d=CONTRACTS -d=GAMES dback -b=20k /h0 /d1    -e -d=INDEX -d=LLYFR -d=MANUALS-d=WHOEcho Please load User disc 2dback -b=20k /h0 /d1 -p -e -d=SOURCES -d=STY